                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "id": "BTSxN",
                          "layoutPosition": "absolute",
                          "x": 4,
                          "y": -59,
                          "name": "text",
                          "fill": "$text-primary",
                          "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                          "width": 858,
                          "content": "backend, before you merge the drain, keep held out of the entry type for now. qa is already writing the test for the stopped case, and frontend draws the queued row from { from, to, text, since, position } exactly as you sent it, so any new field breaks both of them at once. I want three things in the same pull request. First, a stopped agent keeps its queue: nothing is dropped, and starting it delivers what waited, oldest first. Second, the count on an agent's row comes from the bus and not from the renderer, so the two can never disagree about how many messages wait. Third, a message to the whole room lands in each queue on its own, and each copy says who it was for. When that is in, tell frontend so it can wire the row, and tell qa in which order to test the three. If the second one fights the current store, say so here before you work around it: I would rather change the store than keep two counts. And if any of this is already done on your branch, point me at the commit rather than doing it twice. One more thing about the tests: the stopped case needs a real restart in the sandbox, not a mock of the bus, or it proves nothing about the queue surviving the restart. Once the three are in, write a short note in the room saying what changed in the entry, what the renderer now reads, and what qa should seed, so nobody has to rebuild it from the diff. If the note and the code disagree, the code is wrong until one of you says otherwise here. Last, keep the old queue readable for one release: anything still waiting in it when people update should be delivered, not lost, even if it is delivered in the old order.",
